    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **James Wood** anchor the Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup as our most reliable options. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) benefits from a favorable matchup against Montero, who carries a shaky 4.45 xERA, and his placement in the heart of the order provides the volume required for a high-ceiling performance. Even more compelling is **James Wood** (72.30); while his park factor is slightly negative, he faces the worst starting pitcher of the day in Snelling (13.50 xERA). His position at the top of the lineup combined with elite xwOBA metrics makes him a must-start and a prime candidate for a multi-hit effort. We are also leaning on **Josh Naylor** (66.42) and **Seiya Suzuki** (60.04), both of whom exploit favorable SP skill matchups and, in the case of **Seiya Suzuki**, a distinct speed advantage against a pitcher with a slow delivery, raising his floor for stolen base potential.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Kyle Karros**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a score of 40.02. Despite a boost from platoon splits, **Kyle Karros** was ultimately kept out of the utility slot to prioritize the higher efficiency of **Josh Naylor** and the stability of our starting infield. Conversely, **Travis Bazzana** fell just below our strict 40.0 floor, not only due to a poor left-on-left platoon projection but also a 42% rain risk that introduces unnecessary volatility. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Corbin Carroll** and **Gabriel Moreno**, both of whom are dampened by the presence of McLean (2.93 xERA) on the mound; while **Corbin Carroll** remains a start due to his speed profile, we acknowledge the threat posed by an elite catcher pop-time (1.87s) that could suppress his base-stealing opportunities tonight.",
